Don't stop looking for a player who never came out|They're still playing|A father stops looking for his son in hide and seek; the son is still hiding, and the game never ends.|FATHER; SON; MOTHER|A house with many rooms|It was getting late|The son is still playing|The house, the son's voice, the father's hand|routine|sad
# Lost things
Never keep a lost toy you find|The child finds you|A man finds a lost toy on the street; a child starts appearing at his window, asking for it.|MAN; WIFE; CHILD|A street with a toy|He thought it was abandoned|The child died looking for it|The toy, the window, the child's hand|invitation|sad
Don't throw away a lost mitten|The hand comes back|A woman throws away a lost mitten; a hand appears in her house, looking for it.|WOMAN; HUSBAND; THE HAND|A kitchen with a bin|It was old|The mitten belongs to a child who died|The bin, the hand, the woman's face|contamination|eerie
Never ignore a lost child's cry|The child follows you home|A man ignores a lost child's cry; the child follows him home and stands at the foot of his bed.|MAN; WIFE; CHILD|A street at night|He was in a hurry|The child is dead and wants a parent|The bed, the child, the man's hand|invitation|sad
Don't pick up a lost shoe|It walks home|A boy picks up a lost shoe; the shoe walks home, and the boy's own shoes disappear.|BOY; MOTHER; THE SHOE|A road with a shoe|He wanted to return it|The shoe belongs to a dead man|The shoe, the boy's bare feet, the door|contamination|eerie
Never return a lost wallet without checking the name|The name is yours|A woman returns a lost wallet without checking the name; the wallet belongs to her future self, and her ID is inside.|WOMAN; HUSBAND; STRANGER|A street with a wallet|She wanted to be honest|The wallet is from the future|The wallet, the woman's ID, the stranger's hand|witness|eerie
Don't keep a lost letter|It was meant for you|A man keeps a lost letter he finds; the letter is addressed to him, and it describes his death.|MAN; WIFE; THE LETTER|A park bench with a letter|He was curious|The letter is from his future|The letter, the man's name, the park|witness|eerie
Never leave a lost child alone|The child leaves with you|A woman leaves a lost child alone at a bus stop; the child follows her home and calls her mother.|WOMAN; CHILD; HUSBAND|A bus stop with a child|She had to catch her bus|The child has been waiting for a mother|The bus stop, the child, the woman's door|invitation|sad
Don't take a lost doll home|The doll takes you|A girl takes a lost doll home; the doll starts taking her things, and then her place in bed.|GIRL; MOTHER; THE DOLL|A street with a doll|She felt sorry for it|The doll is swapping places with her|The doll, the girl's bed, the mother's face|mimicry|eerie
Never ask a lost child their name|They give you yours|A man asks a lost child their name; the child says his name, and the man can't remember his own.|MAN; CHILD; WIFE|A market with a child|He wanted to help|The child is the man's future self|The market, the child's smile, the man's blank face|mimicry|eerie
Don't look for something you lost in a graveyard|It finds you|A woman looks for a lost ring in a graveyard; she finds it on a grave, and the grave has her name.|WOMAN; HUSBAND; THE GRAVE|A graveyard at dusk|She dropped it there|The grave is her own|The ring, the grave, the woman's hand|departure|eerie
